Thread Lifecycle:
1) New State: When Thread gets just created in Java then it's in new State.
For eg: MyThread thread = new MyThread(); // Here MyThread is extending Thread class.
When we initialise the thread then thread gets created in Java. On OS Level, thread gets created when start is called on it.

2.1) Runnable State: Thread is ready to run and waiting for CPU Time.
2.2) Running State: Thread gets the CPU Time and is executing the code now. Once there is context switch and CPU is giving time
other thread then this thread again goes in Runnable state.

Both Runnable and Running state are usually combined and is being called as one state(Runnable).

In Runnable state, a given Thread can put the Monitor lock.

3) Blocked: When a thread is in Runnable(combined state) it can go in blocked state in multiple scenarios.
 3.1) I/O Operation: When the thread is performing our logic and there is some I/O work for suppose, it has to read some data from
     the db so thread is in the blocked state. Once I/O is done it goes back to Runnable state.
 3.2) Lock Scenario: When the thread is performing out logic and our logic has to take lock on some resouce. But lock is already
      been taken on it by any other thread/resource then our thread has to wait for the lock to get released. Once it gets the lock
      on the resouce it comes back to the Runnable state.

     Whenever Thread goes to Blocked state it releases all the Monitor locks. 

4) Waiting: When we call the wait api of Thread explicitly then Thread goes in the waiting state. Once notify, notifyAll is called then the thread
            comes again to the Runnable state.

     Whenever Thread goes to Waiting state it releases all the Monitor locks.        

5) Timed Waiting: For a particular time, thread will be waiting and after that Thread will go to Runnable state.
   Eg: When we call Sleep api and pass in 5s, then thread will be in timed waiting state for 5 secs and after that it goes to Runnable state.  
    
    Whenever Thread goes to Timed Waiting state it does not released the monitor locks.

6) Terminated: At any given state, we can call stop api of Thread and Thread will be stopped and will be in Terminated State.
               Once Terminated, thread can never go back to the Runnable state. 


What are Monitor Lock???
It helps to ensure that only one Thread goes inside a particular section of code(a synchronized block or method).
Every object in Java is associated with a monitor (also called an intrinsic lock or monitor lock).
This lock is used by the JVM to implement synchronized blocks and methods
The lock is not a separate field in the object; it is a JVM-level construct associated with every object instance
For static synchronized methods or synchronized used on Class level, the lock is associated with the Class object representing the class.

Key Points
Instance synchronized method (public synchronized void method()) acquires the lock on the current object instance (this), not the class-level lock.

Static synchronized method (public static synchronized void method()) acquires the lock on the Class object (i.e., the class-level lock).
